<p>Valencia is the highest-density and most demanded marathon in Spain for two reasons: the course is completely flat, and the date in early December usually guarantees a start line temperature between 10 and 13°C (50-55°F). It is a clinical setting for running a personal best.</p>

<p>But the calendar hides a trap. A marathon in December means the specific training block crosses late summer and the entirety of autumn. The most common mistake we see in amateur runners does not happen at kilometer 30 of the race, but in August. They start the specific cycle too early, accumulate residual fatigue, and arrive in November mentally and physically exhausted.</p>

<p>Structuring a plan for Valencia requires making two decisions before taking a single stride: choosing between a 12-week or 18-week block, and deciding whether to guide your workouts by pace or heart rate.</p>

<h2>The demands of a 100% flat course</h2>

<p>Valencia's flat elevation profile is sold as an advantage, and it is for the clock. But physiologically, it presents a challenge your training plan must address: the biomechanical load is identical for 42 kilometers (26.2 miles).</p>

<p>In a rolling marathon, gradient changes force you to subtly modify your muscle recruitment pattern. Uphills load the glutes and calves; downhills transfer the impact to the quadriceps. That variation provides micro-rests at the muscle fiber level.</p>

<p>In Valencia, you will repeat the exact same stride pattern roughly 40,000 times without interruption. There are no pace changes forced by the terrain. This means specific muscular endurance—the capacity of your legs to tolerate identical impact hour after hour—is just as important as your aerobic capacity.</p>

<p>This is why generic plans that only accumulate easy volume fail here. You need long runs that include extensive blocks at marathon pace to accustom your musculature to the exact mechanics you will use on race day.</p>

<h2>The calendar decision: 12 or 18 weeks?</h2>

<p>The duration of your plan does not depend on your motivation, but on your starting point. Counting backward from the first weekend of December, an 18-week plan begins in early August. A 12-week plan starts in mid-September.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>The plan entry rule:</strong></p>
  <p>If you are running less than 40 kilometers (25 miles) per week during July and August, you need <strong>18 weeks</strong> to build volume safely.</p>
  <p>If you are coming off a solid base, regularly holding more than 55-60 kilometers (34-37 miles) per week with at least one 16 km (10-mile) long run, the <strong>12-week</strong> block is sufficient and will prevent the mental wear of a long cycle.</p>
</div>

<h3>The 18-week cycle: Building from zero</h3>

<p>The 18-week plan is designed to absorb a lack of base fitness. The first six weeks act as a bridge. The goal here is not speed, but adapting tendons and ligaments to the increased load.</p>

<p>The biggest obstacle in this block is the weather. You will execute the first six weeks of training in the middle of August and early September. Heat increases your heart rate, elevates your sweat rate, and makes your aerobic zone paces feel unattainable.</p>

<p>This is where your choice of metric becomes critical. If you start in August, a heart rate-based plan protects you from overexertion. It forces you to drop your pace when the heat spikes your pulse, maintaining the correct physiological stimulus without frying your central nervous system.</p>

