<h2>Control metrics: Pace vs. Heart Rate for Valencia</h2>

<p>As mentioned, the plan catalogue is divided by the variable that dictates the intensity of your sessions.</p>

<p><strong>Pace</strong> is the supreme metric for Valencia. Because it is a flat circuit, the pace on your watch is an almost perfect reflection of your effort. Training by pace teaches you to internalize the exact cadence you need for your goal. If you want to break 3 hours, you need to burn the sensation of running 4:15 min/km (6:50 min/mile) into your legs. Pace-based plans program intervals and long runs based on a percentage of your threshold velocity.</p>

<p><strong>Heart Rate (HR)</strong> measures the internal cost of the effort. It is the safest option if your training environment is unstable. If you live in an area with high humidity, train at varying times of day, or run daily routes with heavy elevation, pace becomes a deceptive metric for preparing a flat race.</p>

<p>If you opt for pace, make sure to calibrate your zones with a recent test, preferably a 10K or half marathon raced all-out in early September.</p>

<h2>The structure of key weeks</h2>

<p>Whether you choose 12 or 18 weeks, the architecture of the specific phase of a Valencia plan shares certain non-negotiable sessions.</p>

<h3>The long run with race-pace insertions</h3>

<p>Running 30 kilometers (18.6 miles) at a minute per kilometer (roughly 90 seconds per mile) slower than your marathon pace gives you general endurance. Running 30 kilometers alternating 3 blocks of 6 kilometers (3.7 miles) at target pace gives you specific endurance. In the final 6 weeks of the plan, long runs stop being just "time on feet" and become mechanical and nutritional rehearsals.</p>

<h3>Threshold work</h3>

<p>Your marathon pace sits mechanically below your second ventilatory threshold (or anaerobic threshold). For that marathon pace to feel more comfortable and consume less glycogen, you need to push the threshold up. This is achieved with long interval sessions (e.g., 4 x 3000m or 3 x 15 minutes) at an intensity higher than your race pace. This is high Z3 or Z4 in most polarized models.</p>

<h3>The Taper</h3>

<p>A classic mistake is reducing volume too drastically, too early. For Valencia, we recommend a taper of 14 to 17 days, not three full weeks. You maintain the frequency of training days to avoid losing muscle tension, but you reduce the duration of each session. Interval intensity does not drop; what drops is the number of repetitions.</p>

<div class="datanote">
  <p><strong>The weather effect on tapering:</strong></p>
  <p>In November, temperatures drop significantly. Your heart rate at a given pace will be several beats lower than in September. Do not assume you have gained an unrealistic level of fitness, and do not try to adjust your race goal downward at the last minute based on this false sense of ease. Respect your threshold data.</p>
</div>

<h2>The counter-argument: "Elites train 20-week cycles"</h2>

<p>It is common to read that professional marathoners structure specific cycles of 20 to 24 weeks for their main goals. Superficial logic dictates that if they do it, amateurs should also seek the longest possible plans to maximize adaptations.</p>

<p>The physiological and contextual reality is different. An elite athlete enters that 20-week cycle after a programmed rest period. They weigh their food, sleep 9 hours a night plus an afternoon nap, and receive physiotherapy twice a week. Their only stress is training.</p>

<p>For an amateur runner with a 40-hour work week, family, commitments, and daily stress, sustaining a high physical and mental demand block for 20 weeks almost always results in a premature fitness peak in October, followed by a plateau and a very high risk of injury in November.</p>

<p>Stress is cumulative, and your nervous system does not distinguish between the stress of 1000m intervals and the stress of a tight deadline at the office. Compressing the hard work into 12 weeks (if you have a base) or limiting the build to 18 weeks with gentle loading ramps is the data-backed strategy that ensures you arrive on December 1 not only fit, but eager to run.</p>

